--- body ---
## Context

**Review requested.** The editor of *Reviews of Economic Literature* (Stanford
University Press, open access) invited the author to propose a contribution.
Proposal sent on 2026-08-06, **accepted on 2026-08-21**: the editorial office
confirms that the subject and structure suit the journal and that the proposed
schedule works for it.

Two reservations to keep in mind: acceptance of a proposal **is not a promise of
publication** — the manuscript will go through the usual quality controls — and
the agreement therefore covers only the scope, not the outcome.

*(The correspondence itself is not versioned: this repository is public and
the exchange is private. It lives in `~/CNRS/papiers/actif/REL_Climate_Finance_Literature_Review/`,
under a dated event directory — `2026-08-21 proposal accepted/` — following the
convention observed on ongoing and published papers: one directory per event,
the correspondence as dated `.txt` files, and `releases/` for kits.
This ticket carries the substance of the agreement, which is what the work
depends on.)*

The open access of the Stanford UP model motivated accepting the invitation,
consistent with the CNRS open science commitment.

## Deadline

Four months announced in the 08/06 proposal, schedule validated by the editor.
Conservative reading: **2026-12-06**. The generous reading (four months from the
confirmation) would give 12/21; do not rely on it without writing to the editor.
About fifteen weeks remain at the September restart.

## Agreed scope — do not widen it without discussing with the editor

Map and synthesise the climate finance literature, on the basis of the curated
corpus of more than 33,000 works (1990–2024), multilingual, openly deposited
with its citation network and embeddings
(`https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.19236129`).

The novelty argument as it was sold: the field has gone from near non-existence
before 2007 to a vast domain scattered across economics, climate policy, energy
and governance — **mapped so far only on corpora of a few hundred to a few
thousand documents**. The scale of the corpus is therefore the contribution, and
the manuscript must make the most of it.

Two movements, in this order:

1. **Bibliometric analysis** — identify the main research streams, their
   publication venues, and their citation lineages.
2. **Narrative synthesis** — for each stream, what it establishes and what it
   leaves open.

**Particular attention promised to the editor**: to what extent the research
literature takes up the accounting controversies that dominate the public policy
debate — **loan valuation, credibility of markers, attribution of private
finance, additionality** — and where it develops its own questions. This is the
part that distinguishes the proposal from an ordinary bibliometric review; do not
let it shrink to a paragraph.

## Available assets

The published v2 of the corpus remains archived at tag `rdj26561-revision1`: 8
sources, 43,179 unified references → 33,344 refined, 38,736 × 1024 embeddings,
1,087,209 refined citations, core (≥ 50 citations) of 2,644 works, citation
coverage 40/47/69 % by period. These figures describe v2, not necessarily the
version that REL will analyse. The working corpus is unfrozen for v3; its
references, including those gathered in the Gavard & Schoch audit, can be
integrated after reconciliation and a scope decision. The REL pass fixes and
cites its own revision of the corpus before computing.

The corpus *data paper* (RDJ-26561), announced to the editor as "to appear",
is published; cite its published version and distinguish its v2 from the v3
revision used by REL.

## Invariants

- **No text or analysis shared with the Œconomia manuscript.** The author
  declared this to the editor for transparency: the two works use the same
  corpus in opposite ways — the history paper asks how the object was assembled
  and uses the corpus only to corroborate its periodisation; the review maps and
  synthesises the research literature itself, which the history paper
  deliberately does not attempt. This commitment is a hard constraint, not an
  intention: on rereading, hunt for any reuse of text or analysis.
- **Cite what we hold.** A literature review cites massively and it is the
  genre where discipline slackens: each cited work must resolve to a local full
  text, guarded by `test_cited_works_available.py`, with the allowlist of
  unreachable works kept up to date and purged when a text arrives.
- **Explicit scope.** The v3 project may take in recent references, but the
  bibliometric window announced to the editor (1990–2024) remains a separate
  decision. Record the years actually present, fix and document the window of
  the REL measurement before producing its figures.
- Consistent spelling, variety set by the journal's guidelines.
- Prose edited in place with the author, not behind a worktree.

## Where things live

| What | Where |
|---|---|
| Manuscript, analyses, figures | `deliverables/literature-review/` of this repository |
| Correspondence, submission kits | `~/CNRS/papiers/actif/REL_Climate_Finance_Literature_Review/` |
| Corpus | working v3, revision and window to be fixed for REL; published v2 kept at tag `rdj26561-revision1` and at the concept DOI `10.5281/zenodo.19236129` |
| Work breakdown | children 0701–0705 |

## Actions

1. Retrieve the author guidelines of *Reviews of Economic Literature* (length,
   reference format, preprint policy, licence) — file them in
   `~/CNRS/papiers/actif/REL_Climate_Finance_Literature_Review/`, in an
   `instructions to authors/` as done for *Oeconomia — Joint production*.
2. Detailed plan of the two movements, validated by the author before drafting.
3. Reconcile the Zotero references and PDFs of the Gavard & Schoch audit with
   the current corpus (DOI then title), decide their inclusion in v3 and trace
   absences, duplicates and exclusions before the bibliometric pass.
4. Fix the REL corpus revision and window, then analyse streams, venues and
   citation lineages on that revision.
5. Narrative synthesis by stream.
6. Section on the four accounting controversies.
7. Author editing pass, then readers.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Author guidelines retrieved and respected
- [ ] Plan validated by the author
- [ ] Zero text or analysis shared with the Œconomia manuscript, verified
- [ ] Full-text guard green on the review's bibliography
- [ ] Zotero/PDF/corpus concordance audited; inclusion and exclusion decisions
      traced, REL version and window cited in the results
- [ ] Manuscript submitted to REL by 2026-12-06 at the latest
- [ ] External registers propagated: personal page `Ha-Duong.bib` **and** CNRS
      roadmap — this is a manuscript, so there is a line
